hub.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2013-interdrought-genemodel/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Quantification of the effects of VRN1 and Ppd-D1 to predict spring wheat (Triticum aestivum) heading time across diverse environments</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/journal-article/2013-jxb-flowering-time/</link><pubDate>Fri, 19 Jul 2013 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/journal-article/2013-jxb-flowering-time/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Wheat response to drought and heat - Using crop modelling to prepare for the future</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2013-wba-drought/</link><pubDate>Sun, 16 Jun 2013 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2013-wba-drought/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Scots Pine</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/project/2012-nsfc-scott-pine/</link><pubDate>Sun, 01 Jul 2012 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/project/2012-nsfc-scott-pine/</guid><description>&lt;p>This project was funded by National Natural Science Foundation of China in 2012.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Breeding for the future: what are the potential impacts of future frost and heat events on sowing and flowering time requirements for Australian bread wheat (Triticum aestivium) varieties?</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/journal-article/2012-gcb-frost-heat/</link><pubDate>Sat, 02 Jun 2012 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/journal-article/2012-gcb-frost-heat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Crop improvement, ideotyping and modelling under climate change</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2011-cimac-crop-improvement/</link><pubDate>Fri, 17 Feb 2012 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2011-cimac-crop-improvement/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Frost and heat limits to the wheat flowering ‘window’ in present and future climates</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2011-wba-heat-frost/</link><pubDate>Thu, 01 Sep 2011 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/publication/conference-paper/2011-wba-heat-frost/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Wheat adaptation</title><link>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/project/2009-grdc-flowering/</link><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2009 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://byzheng.github.io/my-website/project/2009-grdc-flowering/</guid><description>&lt;p>Australian wheat production is greatly influenced by periods of drought and high temperature. In future climate we expect increases in incidences of low or high temperature and potentially in reduced rainfall. This project was funded by &lt;a href="https://grdc.com.au/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GRDC&lt;/a>. We conducted experiments on flowering time and tillering, and assembled datasets from other researchers to build a new gene-based model to predict heading time. The model was validated over a wide range of environments (ca. 4500 observations) and showed substantial spatial variability of frost and heat events across the Australian wheatbelt in current and future climates. In future climates, the target sowing and flowering windows would be shifted earlier by up to two and one months.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>